  legacy code, no async                  -> ThreadLocal with remove-in-finally

CountDownLatch (wait for N services)

  CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(3);
  executor.submit(() -> { try { svcA(); } finally { latch.countDown(); } });
  executor.submit(() -> { try { svcB(); } finally { latch.countDown(); } });
  executor.submit(() -> { try { svcC(); } finally { latch.countDown(); } });

  latch.await(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS);   // blocks until all 3 done (or timeout)
  // aggregate results

CyclicBarrier (iterative phases)

  CyclicBarrier barrier = new CyclicBarrier(N, () -> mergeResults());
  for (int t = 0; t < N; t++) {
      final int id = t;
      executor.submit(() -> {
          for (int phase = 0; phase < P; phase++) {
              computePhase(id, phase);
              barrier.await();      // sync with other threads each phase
          }
      });
  }

Semaphore (limit concurrent calls to an external API)

  Semaphore limit = new Semaphore(10, true);  // 10 concurrent, fair

  Result callExternal(Request r) throws InterruptedException {
      limit.acquire();
      try {
          return externalClient.call(r);
      } finally {
          limit.release();   // MUST release, or permits leak to zero
      }
  }

  // Rate limiting pattern: N permits, 1 permit per call
  // Resource pool:   N permits for N connections

Backpressure (three layers)

  // 1. bounded queue + blocking put (backpressure)
  BlockingQueue<Task> q = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000);
  q.put(task);   // blocks when full -- producer slows down

  // 2. fast rejection when full (shed load)
  if (!q.offer(task)) {
      return Response.status(429).header("Retry-After", "1").build();
  }

  // 3. circuit breaker on the downstream
  @CircuitBreaker(name = "external")
  Result call() { return externalClient.call(); }

  // All three together: bounded queue, fast 429, circuit breaker

Structured concurrency (Java 21 preview)

  // ShutdownOnFailure: fan-out all-of, cancel siblings on first failure
  Response handleOrder(Order order) throws Exception {
      try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnFailure()) {
          Subtask<User>       user  = scope.fork(() -> userSvc.get(order.userId()));
          Subtask<List<Item>> items = scope.fork(() -> itemSvc.byOrder(order.id()));
          Subtask<Price>      price = scope.fork(() -> priceSvc.compute(order));

          scope.join();           // wait for all (or first failure)
          scope.throwIfFailed();  // throws if any subtask failed

          return new Response(user.get(), items.get(), price.get());
      }
      // on scope close, any still-running subtasks are cancelled
  }

  // ShutdownOnSuccess: any-of, cancel siblings on first success
  try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnSuccess<String>()) {
      scope.fork(() -> cache.get(key));
      scope.fork(() -> db.get(key));
      scope.join();
      return scope.result();
  }

  // Structured concurrency guarantees:
  //   - no orphaned child threads after the scope exits
  //   - cancellation propagates to children
  //   - exceptions from children are surfaced to the parent
  //   - ScopedValue context is inherited by children

Decision guide

  wait for N tasks to complete              -> CountDownLatch
  iterate with N threads per phase          -> CyclicBarrier
  limit concurrent access to a resource     -> Semaphore
  producer-consumer with pressure           -> bounded BlockingQueue + 429/503
  fan-out/fan-in with cancellation          -> StructuredTaskScope

Bump-the-pointer and TLAB

  Heap region (Eden):
    [ allocated ][ free ....... ][ end ]
                      ^
                      top pointer -> move forward by object size

  With TLAB (per-thread):
    Thread-1 TLAB: [ .... ][ free ][end]
    Thread-2 TLAB: [ .... ][ free ][end]
    -> each thread bumps its own pointer, no synchronization
    -> TLAB exhausted -> request a new TLAB (brief sync)

Object layout (64-bit HotSpot, compressed oops)

  +---------------------------+
  | mark word (8 bytes)       |  hash, GC age, lock state
  +---------------------------+
  | klass pointer (4 bytes)   |  -> Class metadata in Metaspace
  +---------------------------+
  | instance fields           |  reordered by the JVM
  +---------------------------+
  | padding                   |  to 8-byte boundary
  +---------------------------+

  new Object()              -> 16 bytes
  new Integer(42)           -> 16 bytes
  new String("abc")         -> 40+ bytes
  new long[1000]            -> 8000+ bytes

Escape analysis example

  // Does NOT escape -> scalar replacement -> 0 allocations
  int area(int x, int y) {
      Point p = new Point(x, y);
      return p.x * p.y;
  }

  // Escapes to caller -> real allocation
  Point make(int x, int y) {
      return new Point(x, y);
  }

Four reference types

  // 1. Strong (default) -- never collected while reachable
  Foo f = new Foo();

  // 2. Soft -- cleared under memory pressure (cache use case)
  SoftReference<Foo> soft = new SoftReference<>(new Foo());
  Foo cached = soft.get();
  if (cached == null) cached = rebuild();

  // 3. Weak -- cleared as soon as only weakly reachable
  WeakReference<Foo> weak = new WeakReference<>(new Foo());
  Foo maybe = weak.get();   // may be null after GC

  // 4. Phantom -- enqueued after collection (cleanup)
  ReferenceQueue<Foo> q = new ReferenceQueue<>();
  PhantomReference<Foo> phantom = new PhantomReference<>(new Foo(), q);

WeakHashMap and the self-reference trap

  // OK: entries disappear when the key is no longer referenced elsewhere
  WeakHashMap<Key, Value> map = new WeakHashMap<>();

  // TRAP: if Value holds a strong reference to Key, Key is never weakly reachable
  class Value {
      Key back;   // <- keeps Key alive -> entry never collected
  }
  map.put(key, new Value(key));   // leak

Cleaner (Java 9+) -- supersedes PhantomReference for cleanup

  static final Cleaner CLEANER = Cleaner.create();

  class Resource implements AutoCloseable {
      private final Cleaner.Cleanable cleanable;
      private final Socket socket;
      Resource() {
          this.socket = new Socket(...);
          this.cleanable = CLEANER.register(this,
              () -> closeQuietly(socket));
      }
      public void close() { cleanable.clean(); }
  }

Decision guide

  default (most code)                     -> strong reference
  cache that can shrink under pressure    -> SoftReference (with reload)
  association that should not retain      -> WeakReference / WeakHashMap
  post-mortem cleanup (last resort)       -> Cleaner (not PhantomReference)

// 1. Classic JNI Trap: GetPrimitiveArrayCritical stalls GC/Safepoints
JNIEXPORT void JNICALL Java_Codec_compress(JNIEnv *env, jobject obj, jbyteArray arr) {
    // Suppresses/pins GC while C holds raw pointer -> NEVER do slow I/O or locks here!
    jbyte *ptr = (*env)->GetPrimitiveArrayCritical(env, arr, NULL);
    do_fast_work(ptr);
    (*env)->ReleasePrimitiveArrayCritical(env, arr, ptr, 0);
}

// 2. Java 22+ Foreign Function & Memory (FFM) API -- Safe, Zero C Glue
try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) {
    // Deterministic off-heap allocation (freed immediately at end of try block)
    MemorySegment nativeStr = arena.allocateFrom("bytecards");

    Linker linker = Linker.nativeLinker();
    MemorySegment strlenSymbol = linker.defaultLookup().find("strlen").orElseThrow();
    MethodHandle strlen = linker.downcallHandle(
        strlenSymbol,
        FunctionDescriptor.of(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, ValueLayout.ADDRESS)
    );

    long len = (long) strlen.invokeExact(nativeStr); // 9
} // arena.close() frees native memory; accessing nativeStr after this throws IllegalStateException!

